MOUNT_VND(8)              BSD System Manager's Manual             MOUNT_VND(8)

NNAAMMEE
     mmoouunntt__vvnndd — mount vnode disks

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     mmoouunntt__vvnndd [--kk] [--KK _r_o_u_n_d_s] [--oo _o_p_t_i_o_n_s] [--SS _s_a_l_t_f_i_l_e] [--tt _d_i_s_k_t_y_p_e] _i_m_a_g_e
               _v_n_d___d_e_v

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     mmoouunntt__vvnndd works similarly to vnconfig(8), but it provides an interface
     that can be used by the fstab(5) infrastructure, so that an _i_m_a_g_e file
     can be configured to a device _v_n_d___d_e_v while booting.

     For fstab(5) lines with type “ffs”, the “noauto” option must be set to
     prevent a mount(8) of the FFS partitions before the necessary vnd devices
     are configured.  Also, the “fs_passno” field has to be set to 0 to pre‐
     vent fsck(8) from checking the file system for the same reasons.

     mmoouunntt__vvnndd is invoked by mount(8) when using the following syntax:

         mmoouunntt [options] -t vnd _i_m_a_g_e _v_n_d___d_e_v

     The options are as follows:

     --KK _r_o_u_n_d_s
             Associate an encryption key with the device.  All data will be
             encrypted using the Blowfish cipher before it is written to the
             disk.  The user is asked for both a passphrase and the name of a
             salt file.  The salt file can also be specified on the command
             line using the --SS option.  The passphrase and salt are combined
             according to PKCS #5 PBKDF2 for the specified number of rounds to
             generate the actual key used.  _r_o_u_n_d_s is a number between 1000
             and INT_MAX.  DO NOT LOSE THE SALT FILE.

     --kk      Associate an encryption key with the device.  All data will be
             encrypted using the Blowfish cipher before it is written to the
             disk.

     --oo _o_p_t_i_o_n_s
             Options are specified with a --oo flag followed by a comma sepa‐
             rated string of options.  See the mount(8) man page for possible
             options and their meanings.

             At the moment, --oo is only here for compatibility reasons, but no
             use is made of supplied options.

     --SS _s_a_l_t_f_i_l_e
             When --KK is used, specify the _s_a_l_t_f_i_l_e.

     --tt _d_i_s_k_t_y_p_e
             Specify a _d_i_s_k_t_y_p_e entry from the disktab(5) database.  The
             _v_n_d___d_e_v will have the sector size, sectors per track, and tracks
             per cylinder values of the specified _d_i_s_k_t_y_p_e.  The defaults are
             512-byte sectors, 100 sectors per track and 1 track per cylinder.

FFIILLEESS
     /dev/{,r}vnd*

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     An example fstab(5) entry is:

           /tmp/cryptimg /dev/vnd0c vnd rw,noauto,-k       0 0
           /dev/vnd0a   /mnt        ffs rw,noauto          0 0

     Mounting images during the first pass of fsck(8) and mount(8) is not pos‐
     sible, because the image to be configured to a vnd itself resides on a
     file system that first has to be checked and mounted.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     vnd(4), disktab(5), fstab(5), mount(8), swapon(8), umount(8)

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The mmoouunntt__vvnndd command first appeared in OpenBSD 4.2.

